Hopefully the other points address why you might currently be able to
get by with 200HP just as well as with 1000HP.  The goal is definitely
to
make combat in the late game more meaningful and not make the only
worries avoiding the instadeaths.

> > * Diminishing returns on negative AC.
> > Leaning towards: DR = min(min(rnd(-ac),rnd(-ac)),20).
> - This formula doesn't just give diminishing returns for very low
>   AC; it also changes the amount of damage reduction quite
>   substantially -- for the first 25 points or so, you essentially
>   get about 1/3 of a point of reduction (average) compared to
>   Nethack's current formula.

Well, it's even worse than that because of the distribution.
Consider a DR that kicked in 10% of the time for 100pts of
protection, vs. one that was a flat 10pts all the time.  Both have
the same average, but which would you consider more effective?

>   That means that you would either live with taking more damage
>   at a given AC level, or you would have to pump up your AC beyond
>   what you would in the current nethack.

You're right the second thing is what I would like to discourage.
Your numbers for big ACs are less than what I was thinking.
A DR cap of 10 instead of 20 might fix things and sharpen the
diminishment of those returns.

>   I would be concerned that this change would actually lead people
>   to spend more time beefing up their AC, which I think is the opposite
>   of what you intend.

Yes, but more important is to make it so that no amount of beefing
up AC will make physical combat damage unimportant.  I don't actually
care if players decide they need to spend X hours to make the late
game mostly safe vs. <X hours to make it tedious and boring.

Thanks for the number crunching. You're right, I think the limit
on negative AC boosts has to let you coast up to some decent level
then hit hard with a cap on how much damage you absorb.

